{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block content %}
<article class="prose">
<h1>Snow a month out is nearly unpredictable. The ingredients are less so.</h1>
<p class="lede">I do not forecast snow three or four weeks out directly. I forecast the large-scale ingredients that make it snow on the Sea of Japan side (a strong Siberian High, a tight pressure gradient across Japan, cold air aloft, an unstable sea), calibrate those, and only then map them to snow. The claim is small: a published method applied carefully. No new physics.</p>
<figure class="pipeline">{{ pipeline_svg() }}<figcaption>ECMWF and JMA ensembles → per-member indices → Probabilistic Bias Correction → calibrated index odds (+ what I can see now) → snow-week odds.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>The method in four steps</h2>
<ol>
<li>Two models, many members. I take the ECMWF and JMA subseasonal ensembles and compute, for every member, a handful of circulation indices for weeks 3 and 4.</li>
<li>Calibration. Models have habits. Probabilistic Bias Correction{{ cites(("guan2026advancing", "Guan, Mouatadid et al. 2026, arXiv:2604.16238"), ("mouatadid2023adaptive", "Mouatadid et al. 2023")) }} learns from 20 years of reforecasts how often "the model said 70 %" happened, for this time of year, and corrects the odds. It blends a direct correction (Debias++) with a small regression on recent observations (Persistence++).</li>
<li>Snow mapping. A small statistical model turns the calibrated index odds, plus what is already visible (Arctic Oscillation, MJO, polar vortex, sea temperature, recent snow), into the odds of a below, near or above-normal snow week and of a big snow week.</li>
<li>The gate. The snow call is shown only if it beats climatology and the raw model snowfall in a ten-winter backtest. Otherwise the page says so and shows the indices alone.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Backtest</h2>
<p class="muted">{{ d.backtest.note }}</p>
<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Snow model vs baselines</th><th class="r">RPSS week 3</th><th class="r">RPSS week 4</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in d.backtest.snow %}<tr><td>{{ r.model }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.rpss19|num }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.rpss26|num }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>
<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Index</th><th class="r">RPSS week 3</th><th class="r">RPSS week 4</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for ix in d.indices %}<tr><td>{{ ix.name }}</td>{% for lead in ['19', '26'] %}<td class="r">{% if ix.skill[lead] %}{{ ix.skill[lead].rpss|num }}{% else %}not scored{% endif %}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>
<p>Full tables, reliability diagrams and the windows-of-opportunity analysis: <code>docs/BACKTEST.md</code> in the repository.</p>
<h2>Limits</h2>
<ul>
<li>Skill at weeks 3 and 4 is small when it is real. Expect RPSS of a few hundredths. A "Strong" grade means the odds moved a lot and the backtest says that movement is worth something. It does not mean snow.</li>
<li>Probabilities are rounded to 5 %. I never publish a centimetre number for a week a month away.</li>
<li>The snow truth is ERA5 snowfall over a western-Hokkaido box, checked against AMeDAS stations (Kutchan, Rankoshi, Sapporo, Otaru, Asahikawa). Niseko has no snow-reporting station.</li>
<li>This is a personal, non-commercial research project. It is not an avalanche or mountain weather forecast.</li>
</ul>
<h2>References</h2>
{% if d.references %}<ol class="small refs">{% for r in d.references %}<li id="mref-{{ r.key }}">{{ r.text }}{% if r.doi %} <a href="https://doi.org/{{ r.doi }}">doi:{{ r.doi }}</a>{% endif %}</li>{% endfor %}</ol>
{% else %}<p class="small muted">The reference list is built from <code>references.bib</code> once every entry has passed the checks in <code>docs/REFERENCES_CHECK.md</code>.</p>{% endif %}
<h2>Attribution</h2>
<ul>{% for a in site.attribution %}<li>{{ a }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
</article>
{% endblock %}
